※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4561

※ A lifetime name is a promise to the caller, and the compiler keeps the
※ function to it: what is handed back is worked out from something that carries
※ the name, or it lives as long as the program, or it is refused.  Here the
※ answer is the other parameter, whose lifetime may be shorter than `q`'s and
※ which the caller was told nothing about, so the signature and the body
※ disagree.
※
※ The walk back is the one provenance uses everywhere else -- reading the
※ parameter out of its storage, offsetting it, reading the same bits as another
※ type, through a call that made the same promise about its own parameter, and
※ through what an `if` came to, where every branch has to reach one of them.
※ A reference reached by none of those is not the one that was promised.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6

fn second(p: &u8, q: &⧖x u8) → &⧖x u8:
    p
